      "notes": "All claims are well-supported by their quoted evidence. Tier assignments are appropriate: E7 (Resolution Foundation estimate) is cited as measurable, which is defensible as it is a published institutional estimate of a current tax gap rather than a forecast of policy effect. E8 (HMRC estimate) similarly stands as measurable. E9 is correctly marked projected \u2014 it extrapolates from a partial 2022 repeal to full abolition, and the verdict's plain text appropriately hedges this. E16 and E17 are correctly projected. No tier blurring detected. Magnitude ('moderate') is proportionate given ~\u00a32bn/year fiscal cost and the distributional logic that gains flow disproportionately to higher earners \u2014 not inflated. Time horizon 'this-parliament' is reasonable given the revenue loss would be near-immediate. The rationale is party-blind and does not impute motive; it judges the distributional effect. The direction 'worsens' is supported by multiple independent lines of evidence (pre-existing tax gap widened by abolition, regressive distribution of gains, fiscal cost falling on public services). No false balance issue \u2014 the verdict does not manufacture a mixed reading; it acknowledges a genuine caveat (lower-income sole traders with compliance costs) but correctly weights it as secondary. Source handling is symmetric. No lever leakage \u2014 the verdict consistently frames the analysis through the distributional lens of O14 rather than scoring IR35 policy for its own sake."
    },
